<p>Chapter VI runs from printed page 40 through page 48 and holds the book's last seven pieces: a cat shot on a bad hunch, two outhouse mishaps involving firearms and a runaway cesspool, a skunk's revenge, and two closing essays, "The Golden Years" and "Synopsis," that carry the book's front-matter photographs of the author as a toddler and as a dog trainer.</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="old-dave-skins-a-cat-backwards">Old Dave Skins a Cat Backwards</h2>

  <p>One of the best things about living where your nearest neighbor is far enough away you can't throw rocks at each other is you can pull darn fool stunts and no one is likely to find out about them unless your wife lets her tongue run away with itself or you get drunk and blabber to a friend.</p>

  <p>We have a neighbor, Jim Goodjion, also retired, who loves horses, good liquor, and needling the ladies. All three categories cause him problems which we solve together over a drink or two on occasion.</p>

  <p>We were rattling the ice cubes in our drinks one visit when Jim says, "You know old Dave, down the way, don't you?" I nodded and he went on, "My wife picked up Old Dave's wife the other day to do some shopping in town...seemed like they had no more than left when Old Dave called me and wanted to know if I could come down and fix the stopper on their stool...damn thing wouldn't shut off and it worried him...I told him, hell Dave, you can fix it, just take the top off the water closet and bend that little rod down so the float rides lower...he tells me he can't do a thing mechanical and would rather I take a look at it. So I get in my pickup and head down wondering how city folk survive if they can't fix a little thing like a a float in a water closet.</p>

  <p>"I pull in at their house and he's waiting for me...shows me in and I get right to it 'cause I gotta get back and pull a shoe off a horse...takes me a couple of minutes to get it adjusted just right and he invites me to their mobile home for a drink...keeps a bottle there for our visits."</p>

  <p>"He pours us a hefty one and we're sitting at a little table as he pulls the curtain aside to his left, raises his eyebrows and says to no one in particular, 'Ha, there you are, you black devil, just come a little closer.' I'm sitting there wondering who in hell he's talking to when I see this black cat crossing the driveway...all of a sudden Old Dave jumps up, pulls a .12 gauge shotgun from a closet behind him and pumps a shell in the chamber...I'm trying to sort all this out in my mind when he pushes the screen door open with his left foot, aims and pulls the trigger...that black tomcat turned two cartwheels and stretched out without a quiver when that load of shot reached him."</p>

  <p>"So, here I am, nearly deafened by the blast, thinking it all must be a bad dream. I've seen old Dave live trap mice and carry them across the creek to turn them loose and here he's just blown a housecat to smithereens."</p>

  <p>"I down my drink...he says I can't fly with one wing and pours me another...followed by another to chase the first two...by this time I'm figuring I'd better head home while I can still navigate. Old Dave is already on his way to pick up the remains of the cat when our wives drive in and skid to a stop so's not to hit him...I'm just clearing the door when my boot hooks on something and I fall flat on my face...I'm laying there, flat as a fritter, trying to work my boot out of that damned metal step when my wife walks up and says... "My, my, aren't we a pretty sight, I knew you'd be drunk before I was halfway to town.' Hell, John, there are some things you just can't explain to a woman."</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>The last quotation in this story opens with a double quotation mark and closes with a single one, kept exactly as printed. Jim Goodjion appears, with the same trait of needling in mind, in "Echoes from the Thundermug," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/grown-up-in-creede-part-three/#hopeful-wife-can-be-trying">"Hopeful Wife Can Be Trying."</a> "Old Dave" is given no surname and is not otherwise identified on this site.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="cold-goose-and-warm-chickens">Cold Goose and Warm Chickens</h2>

  <p>Ed Mahoney was on his way back to his farm adjoining the George Perlmutter place. As he turned into the lane that separates the farms he saw George dumping a dishpan off a bank behind their house so he stopped to visit a few minutes.</p>

  <p>They sat on the back porch, George set the pan off to one side and wiped his hands on his overalls.</p>

  <p>"You must have had a tough day George, you look plumb tuckered out."</p>

  <p>"I am tired, Ed, me and the missus plucked and dressed 23 chickens today."</p>

  <p>"Holy mackerel, that's a lot of chickens, did they quit layin' on you?"</p>

  <p>"Well," George answered, "in a manner of speakin' they quit laying all of a sudden."</p>

  <p>"Last night, I was sittin' on the john in my long handles with the flap down, when I heard a ruckus in the chicken coop...I got my shotgun and headed out there...old Rex never hunts anymore, but he saw me pick up the gun...thought he ought to go along and came paddin' behind me.., we come to the barn and I stepped inside...stopped to get my eyes adjusted 'cause we only had a little moonlight...old Rex couldn't see neither, but he didn't stop...ran his head right past the flap. Well, Ed, I hadn't been goosed by the cold nose of a dog before and it scared the hell out of me, the first barrel cleared the top roost and the second barrel got half the chickens on the next one."</p>

