<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="hold-its-tail-and-it-wont-spray">Hold It's Tail and It Won't Spray</h2>

  <p>John Martin rarely lacks for excitement. One early winter morning he was throwing bales of hay on a vehicle for transport to hungry cattle. He plucked one from the bottom row with a gap behind that had served to shelter a full grown skunk. The skunk didn't like begin aroused at that hour, raised it's tail, and gave John a high pressure charge from his knees to his boot tops.</p>

  <p>There's not a helluva lot one can do in a situation of that making except set the bale down and hope the skunk's reservoir is empty. John removed his trousers and socks, pulled his boots back on and went to the house where he told his wife, Bobbie, "I was in such a hurry this morning I forgot my belt." She looked at his bare legs and replied, "Yes, I believe you did, your belt and a little bit more." Then the smell reached her nostrils and she gave him a wide berth.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>"Begin aroused" is printed as shown, for "being aroused."</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="the-golden-years">The Golden Years</h2>

  <p>I have reached the age where the only thing that works on me is prune juice and the only excitement in the boudoir of recent times was last December 17th when the electric blanket shorted out.</p>

  <p>Inez is concerned about my bowel habits. Sort of like she was training a two year old to use a potty. She worries more in the summer when the weather is kind enough to warm a weathered old biffy by a cabin to the west. On such days I like to prop the door open and let nature take it's course. The old dog understands and waits patiently in the shade of aspen trees by the cabin. My memory serves me well on those occasions in the company of a Sears &amp; Roebuck catalog in a far corner and a roll of Charmin under a two pound Hills Brothers coffee can a little closer. I'm not sure if it is the rough seat or the odor that triggers the recollections, but when I'm on the throne I can recall names and faces from earliest childhood. I might stop there several days in succession and if that happens I can be sure there will be a bowl of stewed prunes on the table or a large glass of prune juice by my cereal.</p>

  <p>I would guess constipation has been one of the main worries of Mothers since time began. At our home the tension started when Mom Jo hid the potty and we were directed to the outdoor privy, 50 yards to the north and downwind. If we got cranky our heads were tipped back and a healthy slurp of castor oil was streaking down our throats before we could mount a protest. A large family lived just off the mesa hill at that time. One of the youngest, Harry, had a deep raspy voice. He had been born with it, but his Mother was worn to a frazzle and she sometimes forgot. Little Harry would come in and give a deep throated order for a slice of bread and jam, he didn't mean to sound out of sorts, but if Lizzy was caught up in the washing or scrubbing floors she would pinch his nose with one hand and slop a tablespoonful of castor oil behind his tongue with the other before she thought. Harry made so many fast trips to the biffy his little friends called him "Bullet."</p>

  <p>We did pretty well when Aunt Claudia visited. She was broad across the beam and never missed her early morning constitutional. When we heard the spring loaded door slam and could see her waddling back up the path we would line ourselves up like a relay team to hit the seat while it was still warm. The only other time our Mother felt at ease was when she had a crock of minced meat aging behind the kitchen stove for filled cookies and minced meat pies. No meat was wasted during the depression with necks of deer favored to strip and grind then mixed with fruit and raisins for the brew in the crock. The meat from the neck of a rutting buck deer was faster acting than castor oil. Just the fumes would start a roiling in the stomach. A vigilant game warden could easily tell who was borrowing deer in late November and early December by checking the ice in Willow Creek under the rows of biffies.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image source-doc-image--compact">
    <img src="/images/site/get-grandpa-aunt-claudia.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a heavyset older woman seated outdoors on a rock ledge with two children beside her.">
    <figcaption>"Aunt Claudia," The Bench Warmer</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Caption, in full: "'Aunt Claudia' The Bench Warmer. The dear lady above provided one of the few comforts for our family in 1930. Her ample posterior warmed the seat of our outdoor john when temperatures dipped to 40° below zero making the early morning run less harsh on little shavers." "Aunt Claudia" is not otherwise identified on this site.</em></p>

  <p>At my age constipation is the least of my worries, but the first thing my bride and consultant asks when she gets up in the morning is, "Did the prune juice work?" She should be darned glad it didn't because I never move from the swivel chair by the bay window until I've finished my first cup of coffee and I've never completed that blissful 20 minutes before she drags herself to the living room. One of these days, when she questions me, I'm going to answer, "Sure did, can't you smell it?"</p>

  <p>In summary, I guess the golden years aren't that much different from our late teens. Now, we really do have all the answers, but we can't remember the questions. When I was seventeen years of age I wasted alot of time trying to teach my Dad a few important things about life. He was more patient and agreeable than a drill sergeant who took over when I was 18 years old. Every time I tried to teach the sergeant something he made me double-time it around the parade grounds with a full field pack and rifle. Either he didn't want to learn or I was using the wrong approach. Later on we had a platoon sergeant from New York who lined us up and barked, "There are t'ree t'ings I'm gonna learn youse before I'm t'rough witcha today, an' payin' attention is both of 'em." I think I should have spent a little more time with him.</p>

  <p>What we never forget, in the golden years, is the many mistakes we made along the path of life. Some of the mistakes return to haunt us each day and remind us we should never get too smug with ourselves or ever feel we have reached a plateau where the wind is at our backs and we can unfurl our sails for a smooth journey. We get no free rides.</p>

  <p>What we remember, in the golden years, is that pain teaches fear and fear teaches respect. If a small child touches a hot stove he senses pain. The pain will last long enough to teach the child to not touch the stove again. That is healthy fear. He will respect the stove for bringing warmth to the kitchen and warm bread from it's oven, but he will also remember that it can bring pain. I worry that healthy fear is no longer a vital part of education, either at home or at school. I deserved the pain of every lesson from childhood to this day and the educators I respected the most were those who weren't afraid to generate fear to bring out the best in us. A child's psyche is not as tender as the liberal element would have us believe.</p>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
